<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Echigo Province</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Echigo Province was a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a> coast, corresponding to most of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niigata-prefecture">Niigata Prefecture</a>. In the 16th century it was the power base of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin">Uesugi Kenshin</a>, who ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasugayama-castle">Kasugayama Castle</a> and drew wealth from ramie cloth and mines. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> it was divided among several domains, including Nagaoka, and in 1868 these joined the northern alliance in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/echigo-province.jpg" alt="Echigo Province" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ash_Crow · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Provinces_of_Japan-Echigo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Echigo Province was an old province in north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, on the shores of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a>. It bordered Uzen, Iwashiro, Kōzuke, Shinano and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etchu-province">Etchū Provinces</a> and corresponds today to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niigata-prefecture">Niigata Prefecture</a> without the island of Sado. Its abbreviated name was Esshū, a name it shared with Echizen and Etchū Provinces. Under the Engishiki classification it ranked as one of the 35 superior countries in importance and one of the 30 far countries in distance from the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. Together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kozuke-province">Kōzuke Province</a> it formed the Jōetsu region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the late 7th century, in the reign of Emperor Monmu, the ancient province of Koshi was divided into three: Echizen, Etchū and Echigo. The new Echigo consisted of Iwafune and Nutari Districts and was one of two border provinces of the Yamato state with the Emishi, the other being Mutsu. In 702 it received four districts from Etchū: Kubiki, Koshi, Uonuma and Kanbara. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> extended its territory northward in 708, Dewa District was established under Echigo, but in 712 it became Dewa Province. Sado Province was merged with Echigo between 743 and 752, and from the division of Sado in 752 the territory of Echigo stayed constant until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-era">Meiji period</a>.</p><p>The provincial capital stood in Kubiki District, in what is now the city of Jōetsu, but its exact location is unknown. The temple of Gochikokubun-ji in Jōetsu claims to be the successor of the provincial temple, though its records date only to 1562, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin">Uesugi Kenshin</a> relocated it to its present site. Two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a> shrines dispute the title of ichinomiya of the province: Yahiko Shrine and Kota Shrine.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-clan">Hōjō clan</a> ruled Echigo directly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> period, followed by the Uesugi clan from the start of the Muromachi period to the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> the province was divided among several feudal domains. The Hokurikudō highway passed through it, with numerous post stations, and the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niigata">Niigata</a> was of major importance in the coastal kitamaebune trading system.</p><p>The area became a battleground during the Battle of Hokuetsu in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>. After the establishment of the Meiji government the domains and provinces became prefectures with the abolition of the han system in 1871, and these prefectures merged to form <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niigata-prefecture">Niigata Prefecture</a> in 1876. The province had consisted of fifteen districts, several of which, such as Nakakanbara and Higashikubiki, have since been dissolved.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Echigo Province” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The shogunate gave Nagaoka in <b>Echigo</b> to Makino Tadanari in 1618, with about 74,000 koku.</blockquote><a href="/state/nagaoka-domain">Nagaoka Domain</a></li><li><blockquote>Sendai and Yonezawa sought pardon for them, and when this was refused, delegates met at Shiroishi in May 1868 and formed an alliance, joined by Nagaoka and other <b>Echigo</b> domains.</blockquote><a href="/state/ouetsu-reppan-domei">Ouetsu Reppan Domei</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nagao family were deputies of the Uesugi governors of <b>Echigo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/uesugi-clan-domain">Uesugi clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
